+For a bounded concurrent-refresh comparison, opt in with
+`RELOADSTALL_RR_REFRESHERS=K` (1..=peers-8) and
+`RELOADSTALL_RR_REFRESH_ROUNDS=N` (default 3, 1..=100). This replaces the
+all-peer terminal check with N rounds on the first K non-churner clients;
+without the knobs the existing terminal path is unchanged. It requires
+IPv4 iBGP-RR with the existing disjoint, unfiltered zero-reload shape.
+`RELOADSTALL_REJOIN_METRICS_ADDR` selects the loopback readiness endpoint
+(default `127.0.0.1:9179`).
+
+Initial OPEN tracking must first observe the initial exact table and EoR.
+Each round resets all K fresh coverage/EoR trackers before queuing any
+request and queues every request before waiting for any write or response.
+The `written_us` clock is taken immediately after that client's successful
+socket `write_all`; the time immediately before awaiting queue submission is separately recorded
+as `queue_attempt_us`; it does not prove channel admission.
+Each `rr_refresh_peer` row retains the first fresh base announcement,
+exact table-minus-own coverage, EoR, and the later of table/EoR completion.
+All timestamps use the harness's monotonic microsecond clock; completion
+seconds are `(complete_us - written_us) / 1e6`. These are wire receipt
+clocks, not daemon CPU time or an OPEN/rejoin clock. The first base
+receipt can precede the post-write stamp if the writer resumes later than
+the reader; both raw stamps are retained, and completion must follow the
+write stamp.
+
+Every round emits `rr_refresh_csv` with completion p50/max, non-refreshing
+clients' maximum inter-UPDATE gap (including both window edges), readiness
+samples, live sessions/decode errors, and window timestamps. The gap window
+runs from just before the first queue attempt to the last table/EoR completion;
+1 Hz readiness polling continues through the harness's completion check and
+requires HTTP 200 within 250 ms with at least one sample. Zero observed
+misses apply to that watch interval. Opt-in `rr_readiness_sample` rows retain
+every request's monotonic `started_us`/`finished_us`, sample ordinal and
+success/failure, so actual overlap with the refresh window is reviewable.
+A sample starts immediately before the existing HTTP request and ends after
+its result/deadline decision; it adds no probe, wait or pacing. 1 Hz sampling
+may miss a short refresh window and does not establish continuous readiness. Session loss,
+base withdrawal (including between rounds), own-source delivery, EoR before
+the requested table finishes, missing coverage/EoR, invalid clock ordering, write failure,
+or readiness failure aborts. Event recording during each measured round retains only non-refresher
+UPDATEs for the survivor gap; selected-peer base UPDATEs advance the existing
+bitmap and stamps without adding redundant event vectors. Initial/control
+event recording remains unchanged. The existing hold phase retains its
+bounded-memory behavior.
+Rounds are separated by 10 seconds of steady churn, and native fleet
+cleanup still completes before successful exit. The request sequence does
+not wait for a read or readiness result before sending another request.
